---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// STARTER_CONTENT: replace the hero artwork and its alt text below, then
// remove this comment.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>Unfinished thinking</h2>
  <p>
    A thought that doesn't get to complete doesn't go away. It goes back in
    the queue. This course's claim is that the trouble was never volume —
    you're not thinking <em>too much</em>, you're thinking in a loop that
    never reaches an end, so it starts again. Which reframes the whole
    problem: the question stops being how to think less and becomes what a
    thought needs in order to finish.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those conditions turn out to be oddly specific, and mostly unavailable.
    That's what the semester is for.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second reading of the title is deliberate too. This course is
    unfinished, and so is the research it draws on — half the studies cited
    here are small, several are contested, and the claim above has never been
    tested directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it starts, though</h2>
  <p>
    Not there. It starts with the part you already know: re-reading a two-word
    reply more times than it deserved, having the same thought at 2am you'd
    had at 11pm, shelving a decision because thinking about it further felt
    like doing something about it. The first four weeks are about that, out
    loud, in a room where everyone else is doing it too — and the argument
    above doesn't get properly made until week 5, once you have a stack of
    your own examples to test it against.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    The semester pairs lectures with crits, and the crits are where most of it
    happens. Each one hands you a small provocation and turns you loose on it
    with the rest of the room —
    overanalysing a text message in layers, guessing the ending of something a
    classmate overthought, making and eating a sandwich in complete silence,
    running the same excessive rigour on a best-case scenario instead of a
    worst one. The lectures, held outdoors, supply the research the argument
    rests on. Every class opens with ten minutes of nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>What it isn't</h2>
  <p>
    Not a wellness course, and not therapy. The science here is real and
    cited, including the parts where the evidence is thinner than the internet
    suggests — you will be told which claims are findings and which are
    someone's hunch. The course's own central claim is labelled as a hunch,
    because that is what it is. It also takes almost nothing seriously, which
    turns out to be the point rather than a tone choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone who has re-read a two-word reply more times than it deserved, or
    shelved a decision because thinking about it further felt like progress.
    No background in psychology or philosophy assumed — the only prerequisite
    is a mind willing to catch itself in the act. Nothing you share is marked,
    and you can pass on any activity without giving a reason.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>What happens in each crit, and what to bring.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The outdoor lectures, and the research behind them.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
